<p>A live stream is a room. The scenes below are its walls: what people see while they wait, while it runs, while you step out, and as they leave. Every one is a page under <code>/frames/</code> at 1920 × 1080; add it as a browser source and it is live. And every one is an <strong>assembly</strong> of the same parts — a tally light, a ticker, a camera frame, chat, an alert, a goal, a clock, a card — so a scene the system has never seen can be built from them and still be the same channel.</p>
<ul>
	<li><a href="/content-creation/live/scenes/">Scenes</a> — the stream, starting soon, be right back, ending.</li>
	<li><a href="/content-creation/live/sources/">Sources</a> — the parts, each on its own.</li>
</ul>
<div class="frame-preview" style="--fw: 1920; --fh: 1080"><iframe src="/frames/live-stream/" title="the stream" loading="lazy"></iframe></div>

<h2>The ideology</h2>
<ul>
	<li><strong>One layout, the whole stream.</strong> The screen and the camera do not move; chat and alerts come and go around them. A viewer who looks away and back should find everything where it was.</li>
	<li><strong>The foot is the only furniture.</strong> The ticker: what is on, where else to be. Nothing else is permanently on screen.</li>
	<li><strong>The accent means live.</strong> The lamp, the ring on the camera, the goal’s fill, the dot in the ticker — and nowhere else.</li>
	<li><strong>Dark scenes, glass sources.</strong> Waiting scenes are dark so the stream’s first frame arrives as a change; sources are glass so they read on any screen share.</li>
	<li><strong>Every scene says when the next one is.</strong> Starting soon, ending — the schedule is on both.</li>
</ul>

<h2>In OBS</h2>
<p>Add each frame as a browser source at 1920 × 1080 with “shutdown source when not visible” off, so the countdown keeps its time. The sources go on top of the live scene. For overlays driven by the stream itself — a real countdown, real chat, OBS’s own state — the <a href="https://github.com/imswarnil/Swarnil-Broadcast-Kit">Swarnil Broadcast Kit</a> draws the same ideas natively inside OBS; it is only inspired by this system and shares no code with it in either direction.</p>
